But it can also be used
if you find that modality

00:23:26.183 --> 00:23:28.613
that's kind of a more traditional modality

00:23:28.613 --> 00:23:30.633
of getting a lot of
practice and then getting

00:23:30.633 --> 00:23:32.726
some support videos when necessary.

00:23:33.809 --> 00:23:36.093
I would recommend being
able to sit next to them,

00:23:36.093 --> 00:23:39.313
and work on them, work with it that way.

00:23:39.313 --> 00:23:41.833
Unfortunately we the audio
functionality is a great idea,

00:23:41.833 --> 00:23:44.883
we just haven't had the
bandwidth to do that yet.

00:23:44.883 --> 00:23:46.076
Anything to add Sophie?

00:23:48.073 --> 00:23:51.123
- Yeah, I would say on the Khan Kids side,

00:23:51.123 --> 00:23:54.993
it has been really important
to have that narrator Kodi Bear

00:23:54.993 --> 00:23:57.353
really reading the questions out

00:23:57.353 --> 00:24:01.553
and guiding our younger
learners through their learning.

00:24:01.553 --> 00:24:04.083
And so that there are
certainly audio prompts,

00:24:04.083 --> 00:24:06.853
explaining the question
and helping students along

00:24:06.853 --> 00:24:08.296
on Khan Academy Kids.

00:24:10.583 --> 00:24:14.543
- Yeah, super helpful, so from
Facebook there's a question

00:24:14.543 --> 00:24:16.653
of Sanderson ask, would
you say Khan Academy

00:24:16.653 --> 00:24:19.603
is inclusive to support
students with disabilities?

00:24:19.603 --> 00:24:21.193
So Sander it's an excellent question

00:24:21.193 --> 00:24:23.263
and one that we've gotten
many times over the years,

00:24:23.263 --> 00:24:26.093
I mean, it really depends what
the needs of the child are.

00:24:26.093 --> 00:24:29.753
I would say that there's
an ongoing and process

00:24:29.753 --> 00:24:33.553
to make Khan Academy as
accessible as possible,

00:24:33.553 --> 00:24:36.433
we there's different
thresholds of accessibility

00:24:36.433 --> 00:24:39.233
and we're on that journey,
so I don't wanna claim

00:24:39.233 --> 00:24:42.263
that we are fully
accessible for everyone yet,

00:24:42.263 --> 00:24:46.543
but we're constantly
striving there for students

00:24:46.543 --> 00:24:50.283
who are hearing impaired, we
have subtitles closed captions

00:24:50.283 --> 00:24:52.393
on our videos that, they're
actually for the most part done

00:24:52.393 --> 00:24:54.996
by a lot of volunteers, so
thank you all volunteers.

00:24:55.833 --> 00:24:58.863
We're trying to make the
site itself much more useful

00:24:58.863 --> 00:25:01.523
and consistent and being able to work

00:25:01.523 --> 00:25:05.797
with various kind of aid type apps.

00:25:05.797 --> 00:25:09.363
And we've heard anecdotally
depending on the special need,

00:25:09.363 --> 00:25:12.053
learning needs of the child
that it can be very useful

00:25:12.053 --> 00:25:15.183
depending on where the the child is,

00:25:15.183 --> 00:25:18.203
but we're on a journey, is I
guess a simple way to say that.

00:25:18.203 --> 00:25:22.523
We definitely aren't
as far as one could be,

00:25:22.523 --> 00:25:26.053
and we also haven't done, we've
done many efficacy studies

00:25:26.053 --> 00:25:28.663
on, there's over 50 efficacy
studies that have been done

00:25:28.663 --> 00:25:31.613
on Khan Academy, but these have been more

00:25:31.613 --> 00:25:34.263
with kind of larger population,
so we haven't had a chance

00:25:34.263 --> 00:25:38.093
yet to do it with special
needs populations.

00:25:38.093 --> 00:25:39.813
But we've had some
anecdotal things depending

00:25:39.813 --> 00:25:41.613
on what those special needs are,

00:25:41.613 --> 00:25:45.653
but that's our current state of affairs.

00:25:45.653 --> 00:25:50.473
So, there's a question from
YouTube, Joshua Cataville,

00:25:50.473 --> 00:25:52.643
how should we know what courses to follow

00:25:52.643 --> 00:25:54.263
on Khan Academy based
on what we wanna learn

00:25:54.263 --> 00:25:55.643
at our current grade level?

00:25:55.643 --> 00:25:57.713
Is there a career we wanna
follow, how do we know

00:25:57.713 --> 00:25:59.773
what classes to take on Khan Academy?

00:25:59.773 --> 00:26:03.063
So the first part of it, you
could look at your grade level,

00:26:03.063 --> 00:26:05.433
let's say I'll use the same
example I used a few minutes ago

00:26:05.433 --> 00:26:07.313
of let's say you're a seventh grader,

00:26:07.313 --> 00:26:08.543
you could go to Khan Academy

00:26:08.543 --> 00:26:10.603
we have get-ready-for grade level courses,

00:26:10.603 --> 00:26:12.863
and there you can take
the course challenge.

00:26:12.863 --> 00:26:14.763
And if you do really
well on that, that means

00:26:14.763 --> 00:26:17.923
you have the foundations needed
to enter into seventh grade,

00:26:17.923 --> 00:26:20.963
if you're not do not so
well on it, not a big deal,

00:26:20.963 --> 00:26:23.863
you can go into that course
and then focus on the units

00:26:23.863 --> 00:26:25.823
and the skills so that
you can get mastery,

00:26:25.823 --> 00:26:28.143
so you have a strong
foundation for seventh grade.

00:26:28.143 --> 00:26:30.543
Then you could go into the
seventh grade course, work

00:26:30.543 --> 00:26:33.623
on that at your own time
and pace and you'll be able

00:26:33.623 --> 00:26:37.173
to progress and if you feel
like the seventh grade course

00:26:37.173 --> 00:26:38.633
is easy, you could take
the course challenge there,

00:26:38.633 --> 00:26:40.453
the unit test there and
accelerate through that

00:26:40.453 --> 00:26:42.093
and move on to eighth grade.

00:26:42.093 --> 00:26:44.660
So that would be my advice
on start at your grade level

00:26:44.660 --> 00:26:47.353
and the get-ready-for grade level course,

00:26:47.353 --> 00:26:49.113
if you're ready for grade
level, just move right

00:26:49.113 --> 00:26:50.333
into that at your own time, pace,

00:26:50.333 --> 00:26:51.973
if you need help
get-ready-for grade level,

00:26:51.973 --> 00:26:53.503
if you're ready to move ahead you can go

00:26:53.503 --> 00:26:55.163
obviously beyond your grade level.

00:26:55.163 --> 00:26:57.603
In terms of connecting the
learning on Khan Academy

00:26:57.603 --> 00:27:00.683
to careers really the
second part or interest

00:27:00.683 --> 00:27:05.033
of your question, we don't
have super robust mechanisms

00:27:05.033 --> 00:27:06.843
for that right now.

00:27:06.843 --> 00:27:09.123
I would say and feel
free to add what career

00:27:09.123 --> 00:27:09.956
you're thinking about,

00:27:09.956 --> 00:27:11.693
I could give you more specific advice.

00:27:11.693 --> 00:27:16.233
But I will tell you that
pretty much any career

00:27:16.233 --> 00:27:19.723
that you go into, people
are going ti want to ensure

00:27:19.723 --> 00:27:23.733
that you have strong analytical
and critical thinking skills

00:27:23.733 --> 00:27:25.503
and it isn't just that
people are trying to give you

00:27:25.503 --> 00:27:27.933
a hard time, strong analytical
and critical thinking skills

00:27:27.933 --> 00:27:30.413
are really valuable in
almost every career.

00:27:30.413 --> 00:27:32.633
And it is true that
there are certain careers

00:27:32.633 --> 00:27:34.753
where you actually might
solve a system of equations

00:27:34.753 --> 00:27:36.653
or actually might solve a quadratic.

00:27:36.653 --> 00:27:38.913
I actually did find myself
doing both of those things

00:27:38.913 --> 00:27:40.893
every now and then when I
used to work in finance,

00:27:40.893 --> 00:27:42.963
and when I used to work
in computer science.

00:27:42.963 --> 00:27:46.153
But the ability to learn these
things and think through it

00:27:46.153 --> 00:27:48.973
and deduce it and really
understand it are skills

00:27:48.973 --> 00:27:51.113
that are portable to almost any field,

00:27:51.113 --> 00:27:53.373
and that's why so many folks
I would say especially care

00:27:53.373 --> 00:27:55.483
about learning things like uh algebra.

00:27:55.483 --> 00:27:58.803
I remember one of the early
supporters of Khan Academy ran

00:27:58.803 --> 00:28:00.453
a large restaurant chain, very large,

00:28:00.453 --> 00:28:02.603
a publicly traded company,
and he used to say

00:28:02.603 --> 00:28:05.833
that he divides his
employees based on whether

00:28:05.833 --> 00:28:06.963
they know algebra or not.

00:28:06.963 --> 00:28:10.033
He actually that's his main
deciding factor on whether

00:28:10.033 --> 00:28:12.893
they have the critical thinking
skills and the independence

00:28:12.893 --> 00:28:15.453
and the agency to be
able to become a manager,

00:28:15.453 --> 00:28:17.683
or a regional manager
eventually become an executive

00:28:17.683 --> 00:28:20.993
in the organization, and that's
for a restaurant business,

00:28:20.993 --> 00:28:24.720
that's not for someone
who's a software firm,

00:28:24.720 --> 00:28:28.093
or a engineering firm
where the math or finance,

00:28:28.093 --> 00:28:30.903
where the math or the science
is that much more valuable.

00:28:30.903 --> 00:28:33.383
And that's just the math
side, I would also say

00:28:33.383 --> 00:28:35.723
and that Khan Academy
right now we definitely

00:28:35.723 --> 00:28:39.163
have the whole pathway in math from pre-K

00:28:39.163 --> 00:28:40.793
with Khan Academy Kids, all the way

00:28:40.793 --> 00:28:43.663
through elementary, middle,
high school and early college.

00:28:43.663 --> 00:28:47.103
We have sciences and things
like SAT prep in high school,

00:28:47.103 --> 00:28:49.473
but even the things where Khan Academy

00:28:49.473 --> 00:28:51.693
does not offer things, I
encourage you to get as strong

00:28:51.693 --> 00:28:54.363
as possible, during COVID
try to write as much

00:28:54.363 --> 00:28:57.163
as possible, write blogs,
share things with folks,

00:28:57.163 --> 00:29:00.163
write stories, get feedback,
journal as much as you can,

00:29:00.163 --> 00:29:03.253
'cause I can't tell you how
important that writing skill

00:29:03.253 --> 00:29:04.263
is in your life.

00:29:04.263 --> 00:29:06.883
I do, in pretty much any
career today you're gonna

00:29:06.883 --> 00:29:08.333
do more writing than anything else,

00:29:08.333 --> 00:29:10.663
especially a lot of the
careers that I suspect

00:29:10.663 --> 00:29:12.143
you might aspire to.

00:29:12.143 --> 00:29:14.613
Same thing for reading,
you cannot read too much,

00:29:14.613 --> 00:29:17.103
and I would say that's pretty
much true of every career

00:29:17.103 --> 00:29:19.373
to just be able to read, communicate,

00:29:19.373 --> 00:29:21.313
have the critical thinking
skills going to be

00:29:21.313 --> 00:29:23.216
very, very, very valuable for you.

00:29:24.063 --> 00:29:26.343
So, oh, it looks like Joshua

00:29:26.343 --> 00:29:28.193
is in engineering related career.

00:29:28.193 --> 00:29:30.343
Well, then it's even
more directly valuable,

00:29:30.343 --> 00:29:33.353
you actually will use the
algebra, the geometry,

00:29:33.353 --> 00:29:35.783
the trigonometry, the
calculus, the statistics

00:29:35.783 --> 00:29:38.833
in your engineering, so
it's directly applicable.

00:29:38.833 --> 00:29:42.543
And the humanities and the
language arts are also gonna

00:29:42.543 --> 00:29:43.993
become very important.

00:29:43.993 --> 00:29:46.543
My original degree was in computer science

00:29:46.543 --> 00:29:49.053
and electrical engineering,
I worked in technology

00:29:49.053 --> 00:29:50.923
as kind of the first wave of my career.

00:29:50.923 --> 00:29:53.263
And I can't tell you how
important it has been

00:29:53.263 --> 00:29:55.283
to be able to write, communicate,

00:29:55.283 --> 00:29:58.723
have strong reading comprehension,
and now Khan Academy,

00:29:58.723 --> 00:30:01.353
a lot of our team are engineers, people

00:30:01.353 --> 00:30:04.503
with technical backgrounds,
and one of the top skills

00:30:04.503 --> 00:30:07.713
is obviously they are, they
need to be able to design

00:30:07.713 --> 00:30:09.873
and write code well and engineer well,

00:30:09.873 --> 00:30:12.883
but a lot of it is
collaboration, communication,

00:30:12.883 --> 00:30:14.843
being able to read and
understand the direction

00:30:14.843 --> 00:30:15.676
of the organization.

00:30:15.676 --> 00:30:19.723
So, all of this stuff really matters

00:30:19.723 --> 00:30:21.633
especially if you're going
to become an engineer,

00:30:21.633 --> 00:30:24.083
but pretty much any career.

00:30:24.083 --> 00:30:27.503
So let's see, if other
questions is Facebook.

00:30:27.503 --> 00:30:31.623
Lee Lam asks, if students are
behind because of the pandemic

00:30:31.623 --> 00:30:32.803
how are they gonna manage their time

00:30:32.803 --> 00:30:35.063
to do grade level assignments
homework and assignments

00:30:35.063 --> 00:30:36.413
they are behind in?

00:30:36.413 --> 00:30:37.623
I think each student will need

00:30:37.623 --> 00:30:41.383
an individualized learning
plan with organizational,

00:30:41.383 --> 00:30:43.453
learning path with organizational skills.

00:30:43.453 --> 00:30:46.123
I agree with you Lee, I actually
think what you described

00:30:46.123 --> 00:30:48.933
is actually in any year,
even if we weren't dealing

00:30:48.933 --> 00:30:51.403
with a pandemic, we know that
kids show up the first day

00:30:51.403 --> 00:30:54.383
of school with at all different levels.

00:30:54.383 --> 00:30:56.273
And in the ideal world we would be able

00:30:56.273 --> 00:30:59.333
to personalize the pace,
the scope, what we cover

00:30:59.333 --> 00:31:01.303
to their individual needs,
make sure they can fill in

00:31:01.303 --> 00:31:03.803
their gaps, and that's why
so much of our resources

00:31:03.803 --> 00:31:07.473
at Khan Academy are focused
on not only creating content,

00:31:07.473 --> 00:31:10.413
not only creating exercises
and videos that are helpful,

00:31:10.413 --> 00:31:14.013
but creating software that
students know how to navigate it

00:31:14.013 --> 00:31:16.113
and navigate at their own time and pace,

00:31:16.113 --> 00:31:18.563
they get different levels
of mastery on the skills,

00:31:18.563 --> 00:31:21.873
parents and teachers can
monitor where those kids are.

00:31:21.873 --> 00:31:23.743
And that's also why we
created these things,

00:31:23.743 --> 00:31:25.343
these get-ready-for grade level courses.

00:31:25.343 --> 00:31:28.673
We've always seen really
good results when teachers

00:31:28.673 --> 00:31:32.523
have students start at the
basics on Khan Academy,

00:31:32.523 --> 00:31:34.533
fill in all their gaps
and get to grade level,

00:31:34.533 --> 00:31:36.263
and they get-ready-for grade level course

00:31:36.263 --> 00:31:38.693
has really streamlined that process.

00:31:38.693 --> 00:31:41.013
So we're hoping that
especially in this pandemic,

00:31:41.013 --> 00:31:43.683
but even beyond this pandemic
that Khan Academy really

00:31:43.683 --> 00:31:47.103
is the place that people go
to for free, non-commercial,

00:31:47.103 --> 00:31:50.903
not-for-profit but really
world-class personalized practice

00:31:50.903 --> 00:31:54.933
with immediate feedback that
has other supports, videos,

00:31:54.933 --> 00:31:59.933
hints, solutions, information
to adults to help that student

00:32:00.053 --> 00:32:02.793
so that kids can learn at their
own time and pace, fill in

00:32:02.793 --> 00:32:04.686
any gaps they need and then keep,

00:32:05.619 --> 00:32:07.443
and then to keep going.

